#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193682} :activity/id #uuid "e57b078d-2a16-5382-988b-203a38260e4a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048346 :topic/id #uuid "977d7a77-a9aa-53d4-9b35-0ca8240c298e" :topic/math-academy-id 4124} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193506 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192685 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192390 :question/id #uuid "3ad1cf25-eb36-5946-9e06-16896691aaea" :question/math-academy-id "e-10112"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea184595-346e-5c0c-bb95-8779d73f6f6b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048922} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051082}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192981} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193192} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443709} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443711} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443713} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443716} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443720}] :knowledge-point/title "Log-Likelihood Functions for Bernoulli Random Variables"} :step/id #uuid "d5b9252a-b7f5-52fe-a10d-b66f5fe665fb" :step/math-academy-id 38134 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193683}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193682 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193898 :tutorial/content "Recall that if $X_1, \\: X_2, \\: \\ldots, \\: X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x; \\theta),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, \\: x_2, \\: \\ldots, \\: x_n$ is \n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = \\prod_{i=1}^n f(x_i;\\,\\theta).\n$$\n\nFor example, if \n\n$$\nX_1,\\quad X_2,\\quad X_3,\\quad X_4,\\quad X_5,\\quad X_6\n$$\n\nis an I.I.D random sample with $X_i\\sim \\text{Bernoulli}(\\theta),$ where $\\theta$ is the unknown probability of success, then the likelihood function of the sample \n\n$$\nx_1=0, \\quad x_2=1, \\quad x_3=1, \\quad x_4=0, \\quad x_5=1, \\quad x_6=1\n$$\n\n \n\nis given by \n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = \\theta^{4}(1-\\theta)^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe likelihood function contains lots of products, which can be cumbersome to work with. To make things easier, we convert the products into sums by finding the natural logarithm of $L(\\theta),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(L(\\theta )) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta )) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{1};\\,\\theta )\\cdot f({x}_{2};\\,\\theta )⋯f({x}_{n};\\,\\theta )) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{1};\\,\\theta ))+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{2};\\,\\theta ))+⋯+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{n};\\,\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis new function $l(\\theta)$ is called the **log-likelihood function** of the sample.\n\nFor our Bernoulli example above, the corresponding log-likelihood function is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{4}(1-\\theta )^{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{4})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1-\\theta )^{2}) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0100980e-b08d-530a-9a07-668209e0af44" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7002 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "13d18ec0-09d2-5510-8edc-00b0f1781b94" :step/math-academy-id 42269 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193691}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193683 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192795 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192519 :question/id #uuid "4d597364-c8aa-5848-843b-bfc870ebe737" :question/math-academy-id "e-11119"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50df0342-4c06-5c50-90ae-812403a69cee" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050651}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192982} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193193} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443710} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443712} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443718} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443719} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443721} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483424} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515557} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515592}] :knowledge-point/title "Log-Likelihood Functions for Binomial Random Variables"} :step/id #uuid "012bca18-0bfe-527e-a3ee-dcfee75eafd8" :step/math-academy-id 42275 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193684}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193684 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192796 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192520 :question/id #uuid "5df5188f-3dfe-599f-b6f3-16b93c433ab1" :question/math-academy-id "e-11120"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0ab99b4-70b8-5638-bbad-763ef925021c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049358}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192983} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193194} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443714} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443715} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443717} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443722} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443723} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483374} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483408} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515551} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515572}] :knowledge-point/title "Log-Likelihood Functions for Poisson Random Variables"} :step/id #uuid "f297c08f-8ed0-5ea9-a51f-0fdf821d69d8" :step/math-academy-id 42276} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193691 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193901 :tutorial/content "The graph of the likelihood function \n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = \\theta^4(1\\!-\\!\\theta)^2\n$$\n\nfor the Bernoulli distribution we saw earlier is shown below. \n\n![](images/28/28c73749a566d5531ef2f13c5b01bd1d26ced28e19749abf056841492fb497f5.png)\n\nThe graph of the corresponding log-likelihood function\n\n$$\nl(\\theta) = 4 \\ln \\theta + 2 \\ln(1-\\theta)\n$$\n\nis shown below.\n\n![](images/f7/f7f0ba3147504869e162dd371f98d5d6c492e89345f80c75037bdc82a6006d1c.png)\n\nAlthough the graphs look quite different, they both have a maximum at the same point $\\theta = \\widehat{\\,\\theta\\,}.$ This will become important later. \n\nLet's see more examples of finding the log-likelihood functions for different distributions and samples." :tutorial/id #uuid "7fa77114-72fa-5d0d-89f5-c85ec7793285" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7015 :tutorial/title "Likelihood vs Log-Likelihood"} :step/id #uuid "46ba308c-f318-5b7c-a01b-76e427c7a010" :step/math-academy-id 42317 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193506}}] :activity/title "Log-Likelihood Functions for Discrete Probability Distributions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186192933}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483493} :activity/id #uuid "1cf54a38-7312-5d37-92a8-dc89f388d28f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048346 :topic/id #uuid "977d7a77-a9aa-53d4-9b35-0ca8240c298e" :topic/math-academy-id 4124} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483439 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483374 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b3f6197-0b93-51c0-a8fc-1a87b0fc384c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "43" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482866} {:answer/id #uuid "3b8670f5-d19e-53f5-a1e9-7b40322e7c8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482912} {:answer/id #uuid "68a0ae60-a9a2-588a-aa23-b3eff3415153" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "47" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482993} {:answer/id #uuid "a4a21446-5779-5e15-a526-d39dc8f0ed50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "58" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483091} {:answer/id #uuid "bc56eca9-6be3-5f0a-a094-0678f9e9170c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "51" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483128}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483128} :answer-field/id #uuid "85d1b04e-0566-577b-b438-e45254ccaf6a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483288}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "64469fd0-ae43-5b41-a537-3c86d8172dbc" :question/math-academy-id "q-159843" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3},{X}_{4}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼Po(\\theta )$ with unknown rate parameter $\\theta .$ You're given the particular sample\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=4,\\,{x}_{2}=1,\\,{x}_{3}=0,\\,{x}_{4}=2.\n$$\n\nThe log-likelihood function of the sample is given by\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=a\\theta +b\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{ln}⁡c.\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$\n\n*Hint: The probability mass function for a Poisson distribution is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\theta }^{x}{e}^{-\\theta }}{x!},\\,x=0,1,2,…\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i},\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i},\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=4$ independent Poisson random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta .$ Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,\\theta ) & ={e}^{-\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{{\\theta }^{x}}{x!}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}}{{x}_{i}!}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-\\theta })+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\theta )+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\theta )+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\theta )-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & =-\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & =-n\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}!)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the sample data with $n=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}! & =4!\\cdot 1!\\cdot 0!\\cdot 2!=24\\cdot 1\\cdot 1\\cdot 2=48, \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} & =4+1+0+2=7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, substituting the above values into our expression for $l(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =-4\\theta +7\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{ln}⁡48.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=-4+7+48=51.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "122feefd-544d-5e14-bfd2-c9ac02d4abec" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483481}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483481 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483408 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ba06777-c305-5eb9-bc4b-fa8052f7aca5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482946} {:answer/id #uuid "4d3b5d82-8ea1-5bfd-82b1-7c058b7d2373" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "720" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482950} {:answer/id #uuid "5eea3cbc-3134-5d61-ad72-b78a7587ebc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482977} {:answer/id #uuid "a0b0333d-4556-5509-a952-2fc4a3727122" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "725" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483081} {:answer/id #uuid "f8280fca-980c-52dc-bac8-4baeb8109cb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "731" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483231}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483081} :answer-field/id #uuid "f542d437-7725-5d57-a73d-ba62feecfe4e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483336}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c465be49-1518-5dd7-9865-0bd33016513b" :question/math-academy-id "q-159844" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼Po(\\theta )$ with unknown rate parameter $\\theta .$ You're given the particular sample\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=1,\\,{x}_{2}=6,\\,{x}_{3}=1.\n$$\n\nThe log-likelihood function of the sample is given by\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=a\\theta +b\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{ln}⁡c.\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i},\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i},\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=3$ independent Poisson random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta .$ Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,\\theta ) & ={e}^{-\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{{\\theta }^{x}}{x!}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}}{{x}_{i}!}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-\\theta })+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\theta )+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\theta )+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\theta )-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & =-\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & =-n\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}!)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the sample data with $n=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}! & =1!\\cdot 6!\\cdot 1!=1\\cdot 720\\cdot 1=720, \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} & =1+6+1=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, substituting the above values into our expression for $l(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =-3\\theta +8\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{ln}⁡720.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=-3+8+720=725.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "949e1efa-cf43-5598-9a3f-e2a09ae9e53f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483492}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483486 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192983 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c2ae909-b658-5231-82d1-bfc08a20701f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "306" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189785} {:answer/id #uuid "f80fffd1-3ef7-5f79-8253-5c371476da91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "328" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189786} {:answer/id #uuid "0a6bf0ad-f82d-5cd5-87ea-90314a87da30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "267" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189787} {:answer/id #uuid "0fbfc27a-379b-5db2-8380-5c83a64c3957" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "256" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189788} {:answer/id #uuid "4c37474c-4604-5f31-9804-dafe513c1735" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "294" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189789}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189789} :answer-field/id #uuid "600f464e-4e6e-5583-82f5-58cdc7607c0c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189784}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "1b44bc46-2ed5-5c39-aeeb-2a4f2782bbaa" :question/math-academy-id "q-143473" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼Po(\\theta )$ with unknown rate parameter $\\theta .$ You're given the particular sample\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=3,\\,{x}_{2}=4,\\,{x}_{3}=2.\n$$\n\nThe log-likelihood function of the sample is given by\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=a\\theta +b\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{ln}⁡c.\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i},\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i},\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=3$ independent Poisson random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta .$ Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,\\theta ) & ={e}^{-\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{{\\theta }^{x}}{x!}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}}{{x}_{i}!}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-\\theta })+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\theta )+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\theta )+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\theta )-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & =-\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & =-n\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}!)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the sample data with $n=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}! & =3!\\cdot 4!\\cdot 2!=6\\cdot 24\\cdot 2=288, \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} & =3+4+2=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, substituting the above values into our expression for $l(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =-3\\theta +9\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{ln}⁡288.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=-3+9+288=294.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "aba41867-2e25-52f9-872f-a91e12458e1c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483439}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483492 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483424 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05029119-649c-5ee0-922d-7be3044e0bb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "125" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482818} {:answer/id #uuid "1bef3a60-20f8-5cfb-8010-dc7503bbc503" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "118" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482868} {:answer/id #uuid "a69c00ce-c12a-53c6-b22c-ceb683008661" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "108" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483095} {:answer/id #uuid "aecb29d6-6f0a-5cb9-b349-858797ff2117" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "121" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483107} {:answer/id #uuid "fbbf59dd-b827-5507-a139-4f86e1f3682c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "112" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483235}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483235} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a56878b-d9e6-528f-a90b-8eb05bc5081f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483269}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e74f917e-f8d4-558a-b0ff-23f924d4f647" :question/math-academy-id "q-159904" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3},{X}_{4}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼B(4,\\theta )$ with unknown probability of success $\\theta .$ You're given the particular sample \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=1,\\,{x}_{2}=2,\\,{x}_{3}=3,\\,{x}_{4}=4.\n$$\n\nThe log-likelihood function of the sample is given by\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=\\operatorname{ln}⁡a+b\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +c\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i},\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i},\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=4$ independent binomial random variables with an unknown probability of success $\\theta $ and $N=4$ as the number of trials. Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,\\theta ) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{N}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{N-x},\\,x=0,1,…,N \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{4-x},\\,x=0,1,…,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{i}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{4-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1-\\theta )^{4-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{i}})+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(4-{x}_{i})\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(4-{x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{i}})+S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(4n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{i}}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{1}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{2}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{3}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{4}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{i}})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our expression for $l(\\theta )$ becomes\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{i}}))+S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(4n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\n$$\n\nNow, using the sample data with $n=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{i}}) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{1})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{2})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{3})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{4}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 6\\cdot 4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =96,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nS=1+2+3+4=10.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above values into our expression for $l(\\theta )$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(96)+10\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(4\\cdot 4-10)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(96)+10\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=96+10+6=112.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c476b7cb-9b67-5d92-b4d7-00081ed3f837"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483493 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192982 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84ad1ab8-b93d-52f6-9e80-249eea93163e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "520" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189779} {:answer/id #uuid "887bcd5e-7d6f-508e-a5b4-8b3b1346e089" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189780} {:answer/id #uuid "a34d8c17-92fc-50f4-b8ef-9070e7c92da7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "450" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189781} {:answer/id #uuid "a9aded02-76f8-594f-963a-b0c5e8313a03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "510" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189782} {:answer/id #uuid "75da7d5e-bbc3-5b25-8b9d-6652acaef3fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "480" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189783}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189779} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f926f87-471f-51e3-9db5-133201a435b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189778}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3c5a4f80-f4bc-5f85-966c-3fd9204d967e" :question/math-academy-id "q-143471" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3},{X}_{4}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼B(5,\\theta )$ with unknown probability of success $\\theta .$ You're given the particular sample \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=5,\\,{x}_{2}=3,\\,{x}_{3}=2,\\,{x}_{4}=1.\n$$\n\nThe log-likelihood function of the sample is given by\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=\\operatorname{ln}⁡a+b\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +c\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i},\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i},\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=4$ independent binomial random variables with an unknown probability of success $\\theta $ and $N=5$ as the number of trials. Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,\\theta ) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{N}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{N-x},\\,x=0,1,…,N \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{5-x},\\,x=0,1,…,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{5-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1-\\theta )^{5-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}})+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(5-{x}_{i})\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(5-{x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}})+S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(5n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{1}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{2}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{3}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{4}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our expression for $l(\\theta )$ becomes\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}}))+S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(5n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\n$$\n\nNow, using the sample data with $n=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}}) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{5})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{3})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 10\\cdot 10\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =500,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nS=5+3+2+1=11.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above values into our expression for $l(\\theta )$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(500)+11\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(5\\cdot 4-11)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(500)+11\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=500+11+9=520.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c6a17d7e-1485-51bb-be6e-d6e6626dac3b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483486}}] :activity/title "Log-Likelihood Functions for Discrete Probability Distributions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482800}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515639} :activity/id #uuid "7667169e-9de4-503b-b68f-ed58d102783f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048346 :topic/id #uuid "977d7a77-a9aa-53d4-9b35-0ca8240c298e" :topic/math-academy-id 4124} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515637 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515572 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13b2db6b-e4a2-59ce-aec1-ef7d698cc557" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "46" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514912} {:answer/id #uuid "8592fd8a-a60b-5222-be37-08c4d12b699e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "58" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515149} {:answer/id #uuid "ad3abf38-e119-56f0-b466-68f90093ef89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515236} {:answer/id #uuid "e77a7ff1-b917-56d9-9450-beaa5bf0254b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "44" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515359} {:answer/id #uuid "fe364573-b5d5-5e45-b831-a1dc4fd75f72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515408}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515408} :answer-field/id #uuid "50b0880a-d63a-547c-ac9e-9646cff1bb56" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515438}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "871ccc09-e09c-5033-9027-58208490bfda" :question/math-academy-id "q-159842" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼Po(\\theta )$ with unknown rate parameter $\\theta .$ You're given the particular sample\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=1,\\,{x}_{2}=4,\\,{x}_{3}=2.\n$$\n\nThe log-likelihood function of the sample is given by\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=a\\theta +b\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{ln}⁡c.\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$\n\n*Hint: The probability mass function for a Poisson distribution is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\theta }^{x}{e}^{-\\theta }}{x!},\\,x=0,1,2,…\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i},\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i},\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=3$ independent Poisson random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta .$ Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,\\theta ) & ={e}^{-\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{{\\theta }^{x}}{x!}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}}{{x}_{i}!}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-\\theta })+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\theta )+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\theta )+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\theta )-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & =-\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & =-n\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}!)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the sample data with $n=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}! & =1!\\cdot 4!\\cdot 2!=1\\cdot 24\\cdot 2=48, \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} & =1+4+2=7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, substituting the above values into our expression for $l(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =-3\\theta +7\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{ln}⁡48.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=-3+7+48=52.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "23769111-4f78-5f80-a5ce-c23eb81061b1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515688}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515639 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193194 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "913d6877-2fb9-5a5f-9371-e035641a5329" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "92" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191051} {:answer/id #uuid "152bacff-aa47-51ea-a7b5-7fb58840923c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "107" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191052} {:answer/id #uuid "3bee2cb3-faff-53e8-9928-f4fec6cc836f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "101" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191053} {:answer/id #uuid "59d6da0c-b14e-535c-8319-e8654bb669b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "98" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191054} {:answer/id #uuid "664dde07-5616-526b-8779-8ff7d8c12b71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "105" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191055}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186191053} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed289314-c60f-59bd-b54d-8d68f69bcf50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191050}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d142c298-92b6-5eb8-b79c-25b9f7c9fa90" :question/math-academy-id "q-159772" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3},{X}_{4}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼Po(\\theta )$ with unknown rate parameter $\\theta .$ You're given the particular sample\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=4,\\,{x}_{2}=2,\\,{x}_{3}=1,\\,{x}_{4}=2.\n$$\n\nThe log-likelihood function of the sample is given by\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=a\\theta +b\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{ln}⁡c.\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$\n\n*Hint: The probability mass function for a Poisson distribution is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\theta }^{x}{e}^{-\\theta }}{x!},\\,x=0,1,2,…\n$$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i},\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i},\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=4$ independent Poisson random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta .$ Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,\\theta ) & ={e}^{-\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{{\\theta }^{x}}{x!}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}}{{x}_{i}!}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-\\theta })+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\theta )+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\theta )+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\theta )-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & =-\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & =-n\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}!)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the sample data with $n=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}! & =4!\\cdot 2!\\cdot 1!\\cdot 2!=24\\cdot 2\\cdot 1\\cdot 2=96, \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} & =4+2+1+2=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, substituting the above values into our expression for $l(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =-4\\theta +9\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{ln}⁡96.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=-4+9+96=101.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2cd8a19e-1f39-5c47-b2a8-8e4d001f8902" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515642}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515642 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515551 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "047ee7a4-18a8-58d6-b450-3ca4a9d4d7e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514881} {:answer/id #uuid "10c4fde8-8a2c-5044-81db-2ef8e8bc14ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514909} {:answer/id #uuid "3a95cbe7-f451-5320-9372-bdc4d36f1770" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515000} {:answer/id #uuid "bc2b2632-3760-5a09-ae52-b564a7addc6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515271} {:answer/id #uuid "c562738c-9074-56f9-991a-6abc27300f6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515294}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514881} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee53ea70-06f7-5a83-81c3-442de950c369" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515510}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6141f906-6242-52a2-92a1-6fa0f7b0342a" :question/math-academy-id "q-159839" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3},{X}_{4}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼Po(\\theta )$ with unknown rate parameter $\\theta .$ You're given the particular sample\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=1,\\,{x}_{2}=3,\\,{x}_{3}=2,\\,{x}_{4}=2.\n$$\n\nThe log-likelihood function of the sample is given by\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=a\\theta +b\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{ln}⁡c.\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$\n\n*Hint: The probability mass function for a Poisson distribution is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\theta }^{x}{e}^{-\\theta }}{x!},\\,x=0,1,2,…\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i},\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i},\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=4$ independent Poisson random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta .$ Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,\\theta ) & ={e}^{-\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{{\\theta }^{x}}{x!}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}}{{x}_{i}!}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-\\theta })+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\theta )+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\theta )+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\theta )-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & =-\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & =-n\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}!)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the sample data with $n=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}! & =1!\\cdot 3!\\cdot 2!\\cdot 2!=1\\cdot 6\\cdot 2\\cdot 2=24, \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} & =1+3+2+2=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, substituting the above values into our expression for $l(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =-4\\theta +8\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{ln}⁡24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=-4+8+24=28.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "36d19675-a010-5c2a-8793-7b1c7dc46266" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515637}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515665 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515557 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "270a20eb-2f96-518b-8d8b-0a6f14a6f2ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "92" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514962} {:answer/id #uuid "33016b67-c47d-53ac-b94c-da423f6e0a6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514986} {:answer/id #uuid "8d5ba770-0516-5a66-b241-3645843d77b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "112" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515169} {:answer/id #uuid "939d4573-0cca-5f59-8d62-b98251fd1a56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "104" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515183} {:answer/id #uuid "e8108738-f541-569a-ad5c-f71133abad7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "88" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515361}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514986} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcaa65fb-24e7-5e34-ae1b-a2d7eaa873aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515513}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6aba45a4-2e2c-520c-97f5-9345f709dabd" :question/math-academy-id "q-159898" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{5}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼B(3,\\theta )$ with unknown probability of success $\\theta .$ You're given the particular sample \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=2,\\,{x}_{2}=1,\\,{x}_{3}=3,\\,{x}_{4}=1,\\,{x}_{5}=1.\n$$\n\nThe log-likelihood function of the sample is given by\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=\\operatorname{ln}⁡a+b\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +c\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i},\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i},\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=5$ independent binomial random variables with an unknown probability of success $\\theta $ and $N=3$ as the number of trials. Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,\\theta ) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{N}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{N-x},\\,x=0,1,…,N \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{3-x},\\,x=0,1,2,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{{x}_{i}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{3-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1-\\theta )^{3-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{{x}_{i}})+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(3-{x}_{i})\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(3-{x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{{x}_{i}})+S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(3n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{{x}_{i}}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{{x}_{1}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{{x}_{2}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{{x}_{3}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{{x}_{4}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{{x}_{5}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{{x}_{i}})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our expression for $l(\\theta )$ becomes\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{{x}_{i}}))+S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(3n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\n$$\n\nNow, using the sample data with $n=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{{x}_{i}}) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{2})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{1})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{3})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{1})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{1}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 3\\cdot 1\\cdot 3\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =81,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nS=2+1+3+1+1=8.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above values into our expression for $l(\\theta )$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(81)+8\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(3\\cdot 5-8)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(81)+8\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=81+8+7=96.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "88bd62ec-15d2-5a2e-93d2-79206567236d"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515688 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515592 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3285b65f-b9c0-5ba9-bc03-8954f9ecc650" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1\\,604" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514985} {:answer/id #uuid "3f7e44dd-ef64-5fe5-bc02-5fd88cecfb82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1\\,764" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515009} {:answer/id #uuid "a1b9444d-658f-5c5d-b85f-1d9c6267f666" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1\\,818" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515216} {:answer/id #uuid "bc2a7909-54ad-5cbd-9e86-98ef7dc072d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1\\,698" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515270} {:answer/id #uuid "c2c934c7-75c4-510e-9d9c-2fe6ca8393ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1\\,846" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515286}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515216} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6d8322b-8874-5773-8226-90a9cc0dfa28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515504}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bde496f4-3c68-52cf-9034-88a484da871a" :question/math-academy-id "q-159891" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼B(6,\\theta )$ with unknown probability of success $\\theta .$ You're given the particular sample \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=4,\\,{x}_{2}=3,\\,{x}_{3}=5.\n$$\n\nThe log-likelihood function of the sample is given by\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=\\operatorname{ln}⁡a+b\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +c\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$\n\n*Hint: The probability mass function for a binomial random variable is given by*\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{6-x},\\,x=0,1,…,6.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i},\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i},\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=3$ independent binomial random variables with an unknown probability of success $\\theta $ and $N=6$ as the number of trials. Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,\\theta ) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{N}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{N-x},\\,x=0,1,…,N \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{6-x},\\,x=0,1,…,6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{{x}_{i}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{6-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1-\\theta )^{6-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{{x}_{i}})+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(6-{x}_{i})\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(6-{x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{{x}_{i}})+S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(6n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{{x}_{i}}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{{x}_{1}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{{x}_{2}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{{x}_{3}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{{x}_{i}})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our expression for $l(\\theta )$ becomes\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{{x}_{i}}))+S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(6n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\n$$\n\nNow, using the sample data with $n=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{{x}_{i}}) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{4})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{3})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{5}) \\\\  & =15\\cdot 20\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =1\\,800,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nS=4+3+5=12.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above values into our expression for $l(\\theta )$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1\\,800)+12\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(6\\cdot 3-12)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡1\\,800+12\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=1\\,800+12+6=1\\,818.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "bd709fd0-dfec-5ed5-b548-5cd3d4707f29" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515665}}] :activity/title "Log-Likelihood Functions for Discrete Probability Distributions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514857}]}
